So it occupies 12 bits.

287
00:12:37,300 --> 00:12:39,240
Now, these 12 bits mean the range it can express is exactly what is written below.

288
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:40,760
Its representable range is limited to what is written below.

289
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:41,900
It is just as written below.

290
00:12:41,900 --> 00:12:43,420
It can only express values from.

291
00:12:43,420 --> 00:12:46,340
2 to the power of 11.

292
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:49,140
From negative 2 to the power of 11 to positive 2 to the power of 11.

293
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:54,420
Now, converting this into an actual decimal value.

294
00:12:54,420 --> 00:12:57,200
That is, from negative 2048 to 2047.

295
00:12:57,200 --> 00:12:58,320
A range of values like this.

296
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:00,440
Also, please note one thing.

297
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:02,900
That is, when it takes part in the arithmetic operation.

298
00:13:02,900 --> 00:13:04,060
Since this is an addition instruction.

299
00:13:04,060 --> 00:13:05,960
Before taking part in the arithmetic operation.

300
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:09,400
This immediate value will be sign-extended.

301
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:10,960
We covered this concept earlier.

302
00:13:10,960 --> 00:13:11,740
What is sign extension?

303
00:13:11,740 --> 00:13:12,940
It means that if this number

304
00:13:12,940 --> 00:13:14,640
this eleven

305
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:15,880
twelve-bit number

306
00:13:15,880 --> 00:13:17,440
if its most significant bit is one,

307
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:18,920
then the bits in front will actually all be

308
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:20,400
extended into

309
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:21,840
thirty-two bits of all ones,

310
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:22,840
which actually represents a negative number.

311
00:13:22,840 --> 00:13:25,180
And if it's zero?

312
00:13:25,180 --> 00:13:26,000
Then the bits in front stay

313
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,400
as zero.

314
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:28,440
That's sign extension.

315
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:31,310
So either way,

316
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:33,270
the range it can represent is actually limited.

317
00:13:33,270 --> 00:13:33,830
This add

318
00:13:33,830 --> 00:13:37,310
i is exactly this kind of instruction.

319
00:13:37,310 --> 00:13:41,450
Now, just a note:

320
00:13:41,450 --> 00:13:44,190
even though RISC-V provides

321
00:13:44,190 --> 00:13:46,250
This addi instruction

322
00:13:46,250 --> 00:13:48,270
but it does not provide a subi instruction.

323
00:13:48,270 --> 00:13:49,830
Think about why.

324
00:13:49,830 --> 00:13:54,970
The reason is actually very simple.

325
00:13:54,970 --> 00:13:55,770
It's that, as we know,

326
00:13:55,770 --> 00:13:58,610
subtracting — sub is subtracting —

327
00:13:58,610 --> 00:13:59,790
subtracting an immediate value,

328
00:13:59,790 --> 00:14:01,730
if you subtract a positive number,

329
00:14:01,730 --> 00:14:03,230
it's actually equivalent to adding what?

330
00:14:03,230 --> 00:14:04,470
Adding a negative number.

331
00:14:04,470 --> 00:14:05,750
Think about it, isn't that right?

332
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:09,070
So RISC-V

333
00:14:09,070 --> 00:14:12,190
— we know one of its starting points in designing instructions —

334
00:14:12,190 --> 00:14:13,910
is to be as simple as possible.

335
00:14:13,910 --> 00:14:15,610
So if it can save one instruction, it saves one.

336
00:14:15,610 --> 00:14:17,810
So it didn't specifically

337
00:14:17,810 --> 00:14:19,790
add an instruction for sub.

338
00:14:19,790 --> 00:14:20,670
It's a reduced instruction set computer,

339
00:14:20,670 --> 00:14:23,550
so it just uses addi in place of subi.

340
00:14:23,550 --> 00:14:28,090
That's actually the purpose behind it.

341
00:14:28,090 --> 00:14:28,350
So,

342
00:14:28,350 --> 00:14:32,810
now that we've learned these,

343
00:14:32,810 --> 00:14:34,830
add, addi,

344
00:14:34,830 --> 00:14:36,810
and the sub instructions,

345
00:14:36,810 --> 00:14:37,970
that is, addition and subtraction,

346
00:14:37,970 --> 00:14:39,570
those are basically it for us.

347
00:14:39,570 --> 00:14:41,870
Now, with these instructions in hand,

348
00:14:41,870 --> 00:14:43,550
we can actually use them,

349
00:14:43,550 --> 00:14:46,210
by combining them and picking certain values,

350
00:14:46,210 --> 00:14:49,370
to express some higher-level uses.

351
00:14:49,370 --> 00:14:50,410
So that's how it works.

352
00:14:50,410 --> 00:14:52,210
But as for these higher-level functions,

353
00:14:52,210 --> 00:14:56,870
RISC-V doesn't provide them with new instructions.

354
00:14:56,870 --> 00:14:59,090
Instead, it provides what we call pseudo-instructions.

355
00:14:59,090 --> 00:15:00,650
These are exactly the pseudo-instructions we're talking about.

356
00:15:00,650 --> 00:15:06,150
For example, say we want to negate a number,

357
00:15:06,150 --> 00:15:07,290
to take its negative value.

358
00:15:07,290 --> 00:15:10,230
We define a pseudo-instruction,

359
00:15:10,230 --> 00:15:11,590
called NEG.

360
00:15:11,590 --> 00:15:13,030
That's the idea of 'negative'.

361
00:15:13,030 --> 00:15:14,550
But for neg,

362
00:15:14,550 --> 00:15:15,770
it's actually equivalent to

363
00:15:15,770 --> 00:15:17,690
really just a sub instruction.

364
00:15:17,690 --> 00:15:18,550
But,

365
00:15:18,550 --> 00:15:20,170
note the special thing about it:

366
00:15:20,170 --> 00:15:25,010
in the sub instruction's RSE field,

367
00:15:25,010 --> 00:15:26,390
it places X0.

368
00:15:26,390 --> 00:15:27,790
We know that X0,

369
00:15:27,790 --> 00:15:28,670
this register,

370
00:15:28,670 --> 00:15:30,270
as we covered earlier,

371
00:15:30,270 --> 00:15:31,250
has a special meaning.

372
00:15:31,250 --> 00:15:32,430
That is, with X0,

373
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:35,510
writing to it has no effect at all,

374
00:15:35,510 --> 00:15:36,630
and when you read from it,

375
00:15:36,630 --> 00:15:38,130
the value you get is always 0.

376
00:15:38,130 --> 00:15:39,110
So we know

377
00:15:39,110 --> 00:15:40,550
what effect this achieves.

378
00:15:40,550 --> 00:15:41,490
It's actually

379
00:15:41,490 --> 00:15:43,930
doing one thing,

380
00:15:43,930 --> 00:15:46,750
namely subtracting RS from 0.

381
00:15:46,750 --> 00:15:49,710
and then store it into ID.

382
00:15:49,710 --> 00:15:50,050
We know that

383
00:15:50,050 --> 00:15:54,090
if what's in RS is a positive number,

384
00:15:54,090 --> 00:15:54,870
then effectively

385
00:15:54,870 --> 00:15:56,090
for a number,

386
00:15:56,090 --> 00:15:56,970
0 minus it

387
00:15:56,970 --> 00:15:57,810
is actually taking its complement —

388
00:15:57,810 --> 00:16:01,550
getting its negative value.

389
00:16:01,550 --> 00:16:04,210
If it's 1, the result is negative 1; if it's negative 1, the result is 1.

390
00:16:04,210 --> 00:16:05,450
It's like taking the negative.

391
00:16:05,450 --> 00:16:06,750
That's what this negation means.

392
00:16:06,750 --> 00:16:13,280
This is the instruction for negation.

393
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:14,480
So what else is there?

394
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:16,880
Sometimes we need to take

395
00:16:16,880 --> 00:16:19,480
two values in registers

396
00:16:19,480 --> 00:16:20,580
and, that is to say,

397
00:16:20,580 --> 00:16:22,300
take the value in this register

398
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:24,060
and move it into another register.

399
00:16:24,060 --> 00:16:27,220
In other instruction sets,

400
00:16:27,220 --> 00:16:28,460
there's a corresponding Move instruction.

401
00:16:28,460 --> 00:16:30,260
Now, RISC-V, on the other hand

402
00:16:30,260 --> 00:16:31,940
does not provide a dedicated move instruction.

403
00:16:31,940 --> 00:16:34,700
Instead, it's implemented using another instruction.

404
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:35,960
In other words, its move instruction

405
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:37,540
is actually equivalent to the addi instruction.

406
00:16:37,540 --> 00:16:39,120
As you know, with the i in addi

407
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:40,840
if it turns this last value into 0,

408
00:16:40,840 --> 00:16:41,800
what effect does that achieve?

409
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:45,100
It takes the value of rs1

410
00:16:45,100 --> 00:16:46,220
and adds 0 to it,

411
00:16:46,220 --> 00:16:48,340
and then puts the result into rd.

412
00:16:48,340 --> 00:16:48,800
Now think about it,

413
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,260
isn't the value in rd

414
00:16:50,260 --> 00:16:51,380
still just the value of rs1 in the end?

415
00:16:51,380 --> 00:16:52,640
Right?

416
00:16:52,640 --> 00:16:54,320
So in effect, it achieves

417
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,320
the effect of moving

418
00:16:56,320 --> 00:16:58,040
a value between two registers.

419
00:16:58,040 --> 00:16:58,860
So,

420
00:16:58,860 --> 00:17:00,600
this is another example of a pseudo-instruction.

421
00:17:00,600 --> 00:17:02,740
So the NOP instruction

422
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:03,980
The NOP instruction

423
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:06,560
It is a special instruction

424
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:08,200
It actually does nothing

425
00:17:08,200 --> 00:17:10,660
Now, in RISC-V,

426
00:17:10,660 --> 00:17:13,040
there isn't a dedicated NOP instruction

427
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:15,660
or a no-op instruction to do this

428
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:18,100
Instead, it just reuses our ADDI instruction

429
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:20,560
So how does the ADDI instruction do it?

430
00:17:20,560 --> 00:17:23,840
It uses its rd and rs1 fields,

431
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,840
using the special register x0

432
00:17:25,840 --> 00:17:26,880
And then,

433
00:17:26,880 --> 00:17:28,420
here it uses the immediate value 0

434
00:17:28,420 --> 00:17:29,120
What's the effect of this?

435
00:17:29,120 --> 00:17:30,320
Think about it

436
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:32,140
You take out the value of x0,

437
00:17:32,140 --> 00:17:33,720
add 0 to it,

438
00:17:33,720 --> 00:17:36,980
and store the result back into x0

439
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:39,140
Now some students might say,

440
00:17:39,140 --> 00:17:40,900
isn't that still doing something?

441
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:41,900
But as you know,

442
00:17:41,900 --> 00:17:43,480
the x0 register is very special.

443
00:17:43,480 --> 00:17:44,320
We mentioned earlier that

444
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:46,980
writing into x0

445
00:17:46,980 --> 00:17:47,640
is it meaningful,

446
00:17:47,640 --> 00:17:49,440
or is it meaningless?

447
00:17:49,440 --> 00:17:51,200
It's completely meaningless.

448
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:52,000
So when the CPU is interpreting

449
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:52,520
this instruction,

450
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:54,200
it knows

451
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:55,280
that it really can't do anything.

452
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:56,620
So this instruction

453
00:17:56,620 --> 00:17:57,860
won't do anything at all.

454
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:01,120
So it might just

455
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:02,840
go through the instruction cycle of the whole machine,

456
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:05,440
doing nothing but decoding,

457
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:07,260
and the actual action is to do nothing.

458
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:09,500
Now, some students ask about this instruction:

459
00:18:09,500 --> 00:18:11,880
why did we invent this kind of NOP instruction in the first place?

460
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:13,600
Well, it's nothing more than something in the code,

461
00:18:13,600 --> 00:18:15,440
It serves as a placeholder.

462
00:18:15,440 --> 00:18:16,920
Or in other words, it acts as a no-operation.

463
00:18:16,920 --> 00:18:19,300
Sometimes it has that effect.

464
00:18:19,300 --> 00:18:23,150
So these are some

465
00:18:23,150 --> 00:18:24,730
things based on arithmetic instructions,

466
00:18:24,730 --> 00:18:26,430
we can implement some pseudo-instructions,

467
00:18:26,430 --> 00:18:34,000
extending the functionality of our arithmetic instructions.

468
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:34,460
Now,

469
00:18:34,460 --> 00:18:38,940
after learning the concepts we've covered so far,

470
00:18:38,940 --> 00:18:43,060
especially after studying this addi instruction,

471
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:45,000
some of you may have a question.

472
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,160
Because we just emphasized this point,

473
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:49,440
that is, with the addi instruction,

474
00:18:49,440 --> 00:18:53,200
one of its operands is an immediate value.

475
00:18:53,200 --> 00:18:54,960
But this immediate value,

476
00:18:54,960 --> 00:18:58,200
as we know, since the instruction is embedded within a single instruction,

477
00:18:58,200 --> 00:18:59,240
its width is limited,

478
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:01,200
so the range it can express,

479
00:19:01,200 --> 00:19:02,120
is actually quite small.

480
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:05,240
It can only be between 2047 and -2048.

481
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:09,480
Now, if we want to operate on

482
00:19:09,480 --> 00:19:12,380
accessing a large number

483
00:19:12,380 --> 00:19:16,420
By a large number, I mean one greater than 2047

484
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:20,120
that is, greater than or equal to 2047 and less than negative 2048

485
00:19:20,120 --> 00:19:21,360
then what do we do?

486
00:19:21,360 --> 00:19:22,980
Right?

487
00:19:22,980 --> 00:19:26,240
So in this situation,

488
00:19:26,240 --> 00:19:28,440
to solve this problem, RISC-V

489
00:19:28,440 --> 00:19:29,920
basically addresses how to construct a large number

490
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:32,340
how do we build a large number?

491
00:19:32,340 --> 00:19:35,200
Now, to solve this problem,

492
00:19:35,240 --> 00:19:38,740
the solution RISC-V came up with is

493
00:19:38,740 --> 00:19:40,600
it introduced a new instruction

494
00:19:40,600 --> 00:19:42,100
As we know, a 32-bit number

495
00:19:42,100 --> 00:19:44,100
can actually be broken down

496
00:19:44,100 --> 00:19:45,240
a 32-bit number

497
00:19:45,240 --> 00:19:46,220
can be split into

498
00:19:46,220 --> 00:19:50,200
a high 20 bits and a low 12 bits

499
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:51,820
in this kind of arrangement

500
00:19:51,820 --> 00:19:52,320
isn't that right?

501
00:19:52,320 --> 00:19:53,720
Now what about the lower 12 bits?

502
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:54,940
We can use addi

503
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:56,360
to provide them here.

504
00:19:56,360 --> 00:19:57,560
Because here,

505
00:19:57,560 --> 00:19:59,960
it can represent a 12-bit number.

506
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:02,960
So we can first introduce a new instruction,

507
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:07,960
which sets the upper 20 bits of a 32-bit number,

508
00:20:07,960 --> 00:20:10,660
and puts them into a register.

509
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:11,500
And then,

510
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:12,620
for those lower 12 bits,

511
00:20:12,620 --> 00:20:14,600
we use the addi instruction to add them in.

512
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:16,520
You get what I mean, right?

513
00:20:16,520 --> 00:20:17,980
Just add them in, and that's it.

514
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:19,860
So we fill in the rest,

515
00:20:19,860 --> 00:20:21,480
and by filling in, I mean through addi,

516
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:23,360
that is, adding them in through addition,

517
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:25,280
adding them in A plus B style,

518
00:20:25,280 --> 00:20:26,880
to fill in the remaining 12 bits.

519
00:20:26,880 --> 00:20:28,440
This way, we can construct

520
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:30,240
a full 32-bit number.

521
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:31,040
Think about it — isn't that how it works?

522
00:20:31,040 --> 00:20:33,500
Let's look at an example.

523
00:20:33,500 --> 00:20:35,260
So this command,

524
00:20:35,260 --> 00:20:36,260
first, let's understand

525
00:20:36,260 --> 00:20:39,040
the command that builds these upper 20 bits

526
00:20:39,040 --> 00:20:40,780
— the command that sets these upper 20 bits —

527
00:20:40,780 --> 00:20:41,700
what kind of command is it?

528
00:20:41,700 --> 00:20:43,480
It's implemented with a dedicated instruction

529
00:20:43,480 --> 00:20:45,680
called the LUI instruction.

530
00:20:45,680 --> 00:20:47,300
The LUI instruction,

531
00:20:47,300 --> 00:20:51,120
actually, is short for load upper immediate.

532
00:20:51,120 --> 00:20:59,140
So that's the LUI instruction.

533
00:20:59,140 --> 00:21:02,080
Now, the LUI instruction uses what's called

534
00:21:02,080 --> 00:21:04,740
the U-type instruction format.

535
00:21:04,740 --> 00:21:06,940
We won't go into the details here.

536
00:21:06,940 --> 00:21:09,700
This instruction,

537
00:21:09,700 --> 00:21:11,480
the effect it achieves,

538
00:21:11,480 --> 00:21:12,660
is that it constructs a

539
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:13,820
32-bit immediate value

540
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:16,720
whose upper 20 bits

541
00:21:16,720 --> 00:21:20,100
It stores it on this side

542
00:21:20,100 --> 00:21:22,680
the upper 20 bits, bits 31 to 12, are used for storage

543
00:21:22,680 --> 00:21:23,860
After it's stored in there

544
00:21:23,860 --> 00:21:26,740
bit 12 will be cleared to zero

545
00:21:26,740 --> 00:21:28,880
and then it takes this final immediate value

546
00:21:28,880 --> 00:21:31,200
and puts it into a register

547
00:21:31,200 --> 00:21:32,240
that is, it puts it into this RD

548
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:33,500
for example, the register X5

549
00:21:33,500 --> 00:21:34,320
On this side,

550
00:21:34,320 --> 00:21:36,100
I've written out an equivalent effect

551
00:21:36,100 --> 00:21:39,280
Suppose I use LUI X5 0X12345

552
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:41,160
The value in front here is actually a 20-bit number

553
00:21:41,160 --> 00:21:43,400
So it's equivalent to taking this number

554
00:21:43,400 --> 00:21:44,660
and shifting it left by 12 bits

555
00:21:44,660 --> 00:21:47,260
that is, moving it into the upper 20 bits

556
00:21:47,260 --> 00:21:47,900
and then

557
00:21:47,900 --> 00:21:49,500
leaving the lower 12 bits on the right empty

558
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:50,480
and filling them all with 0

559
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:51,840
Why fill them with 0?

560
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:53,680
They're left there for the ADDI to add on top

561
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:55,320
You see what I mean, right?

562
00:21:55,320 --> 00:21:56,780
That's what LUI does.

563
00:21:56,780 --> 00:22:00,780
The specifics of the LUI instruction

564
00:22:00,780 --> 00:22:02,780
and its effect

565
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:03,660
are covered in our exercise,

566
00:22:03,660 --> 00:22:04,400
which you can go back and look at.

567
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:07,040
We won't go over it again in class.

